Chapter: TlsCvSchemesTest
  cites Encode chapter TlsCert
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 CertificateVerify under the two schemes the public web uses, signed by
 OpenSSL and checked here.

 Until this chapter existed, ECDSA P-256 and RSASSA-PSS were both
 implemented, both tested against published vectors, and both unreachable
 from a handshake: TlsCert accepted ed25519 and nothing else. So the two
 things a real server actually sends could not be verified, and no test in
 the tree could see that, because every TLS test here is a loopback
 against our own Ed25519 fixture -- both halves agreeing on the one scheme
 neither the web nor anybody else uses.

 THE SIGNED CONTENT IS BUILT OUTSIDE CODEX, from the RFC 8446 section
 4.4.3 text: 64 bytes of 0x20, the ASCII context string, one 0x00, then
 the transcript hash. OpenSSL 3.2.4 signed exactly those 130 bytes, and
 .NET verified the results independently from the same SubjectPublicKeyInfo
 DER that Codex parses below. Neither half of this test was produced by
 the other.

 The keys arrive as SubjectPublicKeyInfo and go through x509-spki, which
 is how a real handshake gets them -- out of the peer's certificate. So the
 key type is decided by the parser rather than asserted here, and an ECDSA
 signature can only be checked against a key X509 agreed was P-256.

 We say:

