Section: The Keys

  ec-key : Maybe X509Key = x509-spki w-ec-spki 0
  rsa-key : Maybe X509Key = x509-spki w-rsa-spki 0
  p384-key : Maybe X509Key = x509-spki w-p384-spki 0

  kind : Maybe X509Key -> Text
  kind (m) =
    when m
      is None -> "none"
      is Just (k) -> when k.xk-alg
        is KeyRsa -> "rsa"
        is KeyEcdsaP256 -> "ecdsa-p256"
        is KeyEcdsaP384 -> "ecdsa-p384"
        is KeyEcdsaOther -> "ecdsa-other"
        is KeyEd25519 -> "ed25519"
        is KeyX25519 -> "x25519"
        is KeyUnknown -> "unknown"

Section: Checking

  cv : Maybe X509Key, List Integer, Boolean, List Integer -> Text
  cv (mk) (body) (expect-server) (th) =
    when mk
      is None -> "no-key"
      is Just (k) -> show (tls-cv-verify expect-server k body th)

  other-th : List Integer = [9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9,
                             9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9]

Section: The Guards, Asserted Where They Can Fail

 THE SCHEME MUST BE READ AND HONOURED, and w-v15-cv is the case that says
 so on its own. It is a GENUINE PKCS#1 v1.5 signature, by the right key,
 over the right content -- OpenSSL and .NET both verify it as such. It is
 labelled rsa_pkcs1_sha256, 0x0401, which RFC 8446 section 4.4.3 forbids in
 a CertificateVerify however the certificate chain was signed. The only
 reason to refuse it is the scheme field. A verifier that fell back to
 trying whatever the key can do accepts it, and every other line in this
 test is unchanged.

 The two substitutions are the same question from the other side: the same
 genuine signature under a scheme label that does not match it.

  r-ec-good : Text = "ecdsa cv as server = " & cv ec-key w-ec-cv True w-th
  r-pss-good : Text = "rsa-pss cv as server = " & cv rsa-key w-pss-cv True w-th

  r-ec-role : Text = "ecdsa cv as client = " & cv ec-key w-ec-cv False w-th
  r-pss-role : Text = "rsa-pss cv as client = " & cv rsa-key w-pss-cv False w-th

  r-ec-tx : Text = "ecdsa cv, other transcript = " & cv ec-key w-ec-cv True other-th
  r-pss-tx : Text = "rsa-pss cv, other transcript = " & cv rsa-key w-pss-cv True other-th

  r-ec-wrongkey : Text = "ecdsa cv against the rsa key = " & cv rsa-key w-ec-cv True w-th
  r-pss-wrongkey : Text = "rsa-pss cv against the ec key = " & cv ec-key w-pss-cv True w-th

  r-v15 : Text = "genuine pkcs1 cv, scheme 0x0401 = " & cv rsa-key w-v15-cv True w-th
  r-pss-as-ecdsa : Text = "rsa-pss signature labelled ecdsa = " & cv rsa-key w-pss-as-ecdsa True w-th
  r-ec-as-pss : Text = "ecdsa signature labelled rsa-pss = " & cv ec-key w-ec-as-pss True w-th

 THE KEY TYPE IS THE ONLY THING BETWEEN THESE BYTES AND THE WRONG
 ALGORITHM. Each arm of the dispatch checks xk-alg before it uses the key,
 and with the parser's own output that check is redundant: an RSA key has
 no point in xk-bytes and an EC key has no modulus, so the arithmetic
 refuses on width whichever arm it lands in. Measured, not assumed --
 deleting either check moved nothing at all.

 So the type is crossed with the MATERIAL here. Each key below carries the
 genuine bytes of one algorithm under the label of the other, which is the
 one state that tells the two rejections apart: with the check, refused
 because the label is wrong; without it, the signature verifies perfectly
 because the material is right.

 Nothing the parser can emit looks like this today. That is the point of
 writing it down: the check costs one line and it is what stops a later
 key type, or a reused field, from becoming a signature checked under an
 algorithm nobody agreed to.

  as-rsa : Maybe X509Key -> Maybe X509Key
  as-rsa (m) =
    when m
      is None -> None
      is Just (k) ->
        Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyRsa, xk-bytes = k.xk-bytes, xk-mod = k.xk-mod, xk-exp = k.xk-exp })

  as-p256 : Maybe X509Key -> Maybe X509Key
  as-p256 (m) =
    when m
      is None -> None
      is Just (k) ->
        Just (X509Key { xk-alg = KeyEcdsaP256, xk-bytes = k.xk-bytes, xk-mod = k.xk-mod, xk-exp = k.xk-exp })

  r-ec-mislabelled : Text =
    "ec point under an rsa label = " & cv (as-rsa ec-key) w-ec-cv True w-th

  r-rsa-mislabelled : Text =
    "rsa modulus under a p256 label = " & cv (as-p256 rsa-key) w-pss-cv True w-th

Section: P-384

 THE POSITIVE IS THE LINE THAT MATTERS, and it is the one that pins the
 DIGEST. ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384 binds SHA-384 into the code point, and
 EcdsaP256 exports both ecdsa-p384-verify and ecdsa-p384-verify-sha384 --
 two entry points differing only in which digest they take. A verifier
 wired to the SHA-256 one accepts nothing a real peer sends, and every
 negative line below stays False while it does so. Only this line moves.

  r-p384-good : Text = "p384 cv as server = " & cv p384-key w-p384-cv True w-th

  r-p384-role : Text = "p384 cv as client = " & cv p384-key w-p384-cv False w-th
  r-p384-tx : Text = "p384 cv, other transcript = " & cv p384-key w-p384-cv True other-th
  r-p384-wrongkey : Text = "p384 cv against the p256 key = " & cv ec-key w-p384-cv True w-th

 The two EC schemes must not be interchangeable, and these two lines are
 what says the dispatch reads the scheme rather than trying whatever the
 key can do. Route 0x0503 to the P-256 arm by mistake and r-p384-good goes
 False; let either EC arm take the other's key and the matching line here
 goes True.

 As with the RSA pair above, the key-type check inside each arm is
 redundant against this parser's own output -- a P-256 point is 65 bytes
 and a P-384 point is 97, so the arithmetic refuses on width whichever arm
 it lands in. These lines are evidence about the DISPATCH, not about those
 checks.

  r-p384-as-p256 : Text =
    "p384 signature labelled p256 = " & cv p384-key w-p384-as-p256 True w-th
  r-p256-as-p384 : Text =
    "p256 signature labelled p384 = " & cv ec-key w-p256-as-p384 True w-th

 Every scheme the ClientHello offers must be one this verifier accepts.
 The two sets are written in two places -- tls-ext-sig-algs in Tls and the
 dispatch in TlsCert -- and nothing else makes them agree.

  offered : Integer -> Integer
  offered (i) = list-at tls-ext-sig-algs i * 256 + list-at tls-ext-sig-algs (i + 1)

  r-offered : Text =
    "offered schemes = " & show (offered 6) & "," & show (offered 8) & "," & show (offered 10) & "," & show (offered 12)

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("ec key parses as " & kind ec-key)
    print-line-uni ("rsa key parses as " & kind rsa-key)
    print-line-uni ("p384 key parses as " & kind p384-key)
    print-line-uni r-ec-good
    print-line-uni r-pss-good
    print-line-uni r-ec-role
    print-line-uni r-pss-role
    print-line-uni r-ec-tx
    print-line-uni r-pss-tx
    print-line-uni r-ec-wrongkey
    print-line-uni r-pss-wrongkey
    print-line-uni r-v15
    print-line-uni r-pss-as-ecdsa
    print-line-uni r-ec-as-pss
    print-line-uni r-ec-mislabelled
    print-line-uni r-rsa-mislabelled
    print-line-uni r-p384-good
    print-line-uni r-p384-role
    print-line-uni r-p384-tx
    print-line-uni r-p384-wrongkey
    print-line-uni r-p384-as-p256
    print-line-uni r-p256-as-p384
    print-line-uni r-offered
  end

